azgor wrote:
> This is a collection of YANIs by users of the #nethack IRC channel on
> freenode.net. Please excuse any old ideas that may be present.

Three in two days, cool!

> [1]: Scrolls of identify should be a lot more expensive. Knowledge is
> power. --Grunt

Scrolls of identify are the most common scroll, and thus should not be
expensive.  And their status as THE cheapest item makes them too easy
to price ID (figuring out that first ID scroll is a major milestone of
progress).  But they should not be a LOT more expensive.

I think, overall, it's too easy to identify stuff in Nethack.  It's
good that there are so many ways to figure out what things are without
using the scroll, but there's a sense that there's too many.  (Wands,
with the engraving trick, are way too easy -- at least with dropping
rings down sinks you lose it in the process.)  I'd consider removing
the identify-the-pack feature from scrolls and spells of identify
entirely, maybe making the identify-several effect a little more
powerful to compensate.

> [2]: This is a bug: When wielded a shield of reflection doesn't reflect
> --scwizard

Hm, interesting way of thinking of it.  But "wearing" a shield isn't
the same as "wielding" it, presumably in the latter case it's being
held in a way that doesn't utilize its reflective surface to its
fullest extent.

> [3]: When #dipping or diluting a smoky potion by any other means, there
> should be the chance of a djinni appearing, most likely hostle. I mean,
> who here enjoys having cold water dumped on them when they're trying to
> sleep? --LordMatthius

Hmm, it's interesting to put in a further check against alchemy.

> [4]: A scroll of charging on the ground should untame any q, or u - it
> makes them charge. --SpeedEvil

Interesting idea, I don't know abut *untaming* them though.  Maybe they
just feel the need to jump over it, possibly crashing into the player
along the way?  But that's getting into exceedingly rare kinds of
interaction.

> [5]: new scroll : polymorph. high base price range(350-400). when
> uncursed and on self acts like a polytrap, when given direction acts
> like /oPoly. When blessed, on self acts like you have poly control,
> same as uncursed otherwise. Cursed acts like polytrap but does not ask
> direction, targets self automatically. Confused : cursed targets random
> object(s) in inventory and polys them --Xarann

You can already polymorph by potion, wand, ring, trap, fountain or from
eating a chameleon.    There is also something to be said about there
not being perfect parity between the item classes.  This is probably
too redundant.

> [6]: Continued..., uncursed acts like you zap /oPoly in random
> direction around you, blessed targets {some_armor}, makes it
> "polyproof/shapefitting"(armor that doesn't break when you poly into
> something too big) --Xarann

Why would a scroll of polymorph *polyproof* something?

> [7]: If you eat with a very high nutrition (say >1800) - instead of
> choking, you explode. --SpeedEvil

<cue obligatory Mr. Creosote joke>

But really, Nethack is Not A Warner Bros Cartoon.  (NINAWBC)

> [8]: Have everything on the map change colours when hallucinating.
> (Everything really does look cosmic! And it looks boring afterwards,
> too.) --Grunt

This would require interface changes for those playing with graphical
support, but is a cool idea otherwise provided it doesn't get too
flashy.

> [9]: Some ill status effects could time out gradually, i.e. has only a
> chance of impacting an action related to the numbers of turns it has to
> time out. (For the hallucination idea just listed the map could
> gradually return to normal.) --Grunt

Would be a lot of implementation work for limited results.  Let's hear
how it'd work with the other status effects first (most of which are
already too weak as it is).

> [10]: A used up amulet of life saving should also act as a restore
> ability. I mean, c'mon: If you lose your head, it gives you your whole
> head back. If you lose your brains, you only get 3 points back.
> --TheShrewdDude

Actually, isn't brainlessness the only thing lifesaving doesn't save
from?  Or am I thinking about wizard mode?  Or self-genocide?

But to answer your point, no, lifesaving shouldn't be made even more
powerful, neither should restore ability be made less useful.

> [11]: You should be able to wish for Chromatic Dragon scales. It gives
> you resistance to... nothing, but it looks cool! (When placed on the
> ground, it changes colours. Yeah.) --TheShrewdDude

Nah.

> [12]: Ring of Disintegration: Every n500 turns, you begin the
> disintegration process, much like stoning or sliming. This can only be
> cured by gremlin attack, praying, putting on a bdsm, gaining
> disintegration resistance, or putting on a ring of gain constitution.
> --TheShrewdDude

Hell no, it'd make it too ill-advisable to identify rings by
experimentation.  (This is the same reason smart people don't eat eggs
lying around the dungeon -- the chance a given one will be cockatrice
is slim, but even a slight chance is too much.)

> [13]: There is a portal to ancient Yendor in there somewhere. If you go
> there, try to destroy the Amuletsmith, or sacrifice him to your god.
> --TheShrewdDude

I ---really--- think Yendor should be made a bigger part of the game in
some way, but the idea of adding an ADOM-esque alternate ending is a
bad one.

> [14]: Using wands should require you to wield them. Or at least give a
> bonus for wielding wands. --TheShrewdDude

It would make cursed wands more dangerous, but may ultimately be too
annoying (less so, however, now we have the alternate weapon slot).
But wielding doesn't mean just holding, it means holding with intent to
strike.

Note that, in some versions of Rogue, the proper use for wands of
striking is to wield them!

> [15]: As should markers. --SpeedEvil

Same comment as above.

